"""Form 5 Office Practice (O Level 5090, code to be confirmed), modules 1-3:
The Office & Organizational Structure; Reception, Etiquette & Telecommunications; Mail Room Operations.
Draft content: check against the official syllabus and your textbook. Office Practice is not in the
Board's 2023 report; paper format follows the KKEVO blueprint (check with your teacher)."""

from .dsl import *  # noqa: F403

COURSE = ("office-practice", "form-5")

M1 = "The Office & Organizational Structure"
M2 = "Reception, Etiquette & Telecommunications"
M3 = "Mail Room Operations"

LESSONS = [
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1.1
    lesson(
        module=M1,
        topic="Functions and location of an office",
        title="What an office does and where to place it",
        slug="ofp1-functions-and-location-of-an-office",
        minutes=20,
        summary="An office is the place where an organization handles its information and paperwork. Its main functions are receiving, recording, storing, processing and supplying information, and its location is chosen by weighing access, cost, services and safety.",
        objectives=[
            "Define an office and explain why every organization needs one",
            "List and explain the main functions of an office",
            "State the factors that affect the choice of an office location",
            "Apply these factors to choose a sensible location for a small business",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Office", "Information", "Recording", "Filing", "Communication", "Location", "Accessibility", "Premises"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("A school has a principal's office, a bursar's office and a secretary's office. A bank branch, a council, a hospital and a small printing shop in Bafoussam all have offices too. "
              "They are very different organizations, yet each one needs a place where letters are received, money is recorded, files are kept and decisions are written down."),
            T("This lesson explains what an office is for, and how an organization decides where to put it. Office Practice is the subject that teaches how this work is done well. "
              "Check the exact paper format for this subject with your teacher: the blueprint used by KKEVO describes a multiple-choice paper and a written paper."),
            H("What is an office?"),
            D("An **office** is a place (a room, a building or even a set of computers and phones) where the clerical and administrative work of an organization is done. "
              "Clerical work means handling information: writing, typing, recording, filing, calculating and communicating.",
              title="Definition: office",
              note="An office does not make the product. A factory makes goods; the office organizes the work of the factory."),
            T("Think of the office as the **nerve centre** or the memory of an organization. Without it, the managers would not know what has been sold, what is owed, who has been paid or what was decided last week."),
            H("The functions of an office"),
            KEY("Main functions", "Different books group them slightly differently. Learn these six and their meaning.", cards=[
                ["Receiving information", "Letters, calls, emails, orders, invoices and visitors bring information in. Someone must receive it and pass it to the right person."],
                ["Recording information", "Facts are written or typed so they are not forgotten: sales records, the visitors' register, minutes of meetings, accounts."],
                ["Storing information", "Documents are kept safely in files, cabinets or on computers so they can be found again (filing and archiving)."],
                ["Processing information", "Raw facts are turned into useful results: calculating wages, preparing reports, typing letters, producing statements."],
                ["Supplying and communicating information", "The right information is given to the right people at the right time: managers, staff, customers, the tax office."],
                ["Safeguarding assets and supporting other departments", "The office controls cash and documents, and serves the sales, production and other departments with its services."],
            ]),
            T("Some books also list **control** (checking that work is done properly) and **protection of the organization's money and documents** as separate functions. "
              "If your textbook uses a different list, learn your textbook's list and keep this one for understanding."),
            EX("Worked example 1: matching tasks to functions", intro="A clerk at an invented company, Mbah Hardware Ltd (name invented for practice), does these tasks in one morning. Say which office function each one is.", steps=[
                S("Task 1: she opens a letter from a supplier", text="This is **receiving** information."),
                S("Task 2: she writes the supplier's name and the amount in the purchases record", text="This is **recording** information."),
                S("Task 3: she puts the letter in the supplier's folder in the cabinet", text="This is **storing** information (filing)."),
                S("Task 4: she prepares a list of all amounts owed to suppliers for the manager", text="This is **processing** information (turning facts into a useful summary), and handing it over is **supplying** information."),
            ]),
            H("Why location matters"),
            T("An organization must choose where to put its office. A bad choice is expensive to correct: moving means new rent, new signs, lost customers and lost days. "
              "The right choice depends on the kind of business."),
            KEY("Factors affecting office location", "Know the factors and be able to give a reason for each.", cards=[
                ["Accessibility", "Can customers, staff and visitors reach it easily by road and public transport? An office on a busy road in town is easier to reach than one far down a bad track."],
                ["Nearness to services", "Banks, the post office or courier agents, suppliers, the council and the tax office are used often. Being close saves time and transport costs."],
                ["Cost", "Rent or the price of land and the cost of building differ between a town centre and the outskirts. A small business may choose a cheaper area."],
                ["Utilities and communication", "Reliable water, electricity and a mobile network or internet connection are needed. Without them work stops."],
                ["Security and surroundings", "Safe from theft, flooding and fire. A quiet area is better for concentration than beside a noisy market or a mechanic's yard."],
                ["Staff and room to grow", "Can staff get to work easily and are there people with the right skills nearby? Is there space to expand later? Does the law allow this use of the building?"],
            ]),
            TIP("A customer-facing office (a bank, a clinic, a travel agent) should be on the ground floor in an easy-to-find place. A back-office that customers rarely visit (for example a head office record room) can be cheaper and further away."),
            EX("Worked example 2: choosing a location", intro="Mrs Ngu plans to open a small secretarial and photocopying bureau (invented example) near a university campus. She is choosing between a shop beside the main campus gate and a larger, cheaper room 5 km away. Which should she choose, and why?", steps=[
                S("Identify who the customers are", text="Students and lecturers who want quick services and walk in without an appointment."),
                S("Apply accessibility", text="The gate shop is within walking distance of all customers. The distant room needs transport, so many would not come."),
                S("Apply cost", text="The distant room is cheaper, but the saving is small if there are few customers."),
                S("Decide", text="The shop beside the gate is better. Its higher rent is paid for by the steady stream of walk-in customers. She should still check that it has electricity and a secure door."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Giving only one factor", "A common weakness is to write only \"it should be near customers\". Examiners prefer several factors, each with a reason. Say what the factor means and why it matters for that business."),
            MISTAKE("Confusing the office with the whole organization", "The office is one part of the organization, the part that handles information. Do not write that the office \"produces goods\" unless the question describes a production office that plans or controls production."),
            QUIZ("Which is a function of an office?",
                 ["Growing crops for sale", "Recording and storing information", "Transporting goods to customers", "Repairing machines"], 1,
                 "Offices handle information: they record, store, process and supply it. The other jobs are done by farms, transport and workshop departments.",
                 hint="Think about what an office makes: it does not make physical goods.",
                 feedback=["That is farming, not office work.", "", "That is a transport function.", "That is done in a workshop."]),
            QUIZ("A small shop selling school stationery should locate its office or counter mainly where...",
                 ["rent is lowest, even in a remote village", "customers can reach it easily", "there is no electricity", "no other shops exist"], 1,
                 "A retail business depends on customers who can reach it easily. Low rent matters, but not at the cost of losing customers.",
                 hint="Who pays the shop's bills? Where do they walk?",
                 feedback=["Cost is only one factor. A remote site loses customers.", "", "Electricity is a need, not something to avoid.", "A shop usually benefits from being near other trade."]),
            PQ("State four functions of an office and give one example of each.", "Receiving: opening incoming letters. Recording: writing sales in a record book. Storing: filing documents in a cabinet. Processing: calculating wages. (Supplying: giving a report to the manager.) Any four with correct examples."),
            PQ("List four factors that affect the choice of an office location and explain any two.", "Accessibility, cost, nearness to banks and suppliers, utilities such as electricity and internet, security, room for expansion. Accessibility: customers and staff must be able to reach the office easily, otherwise business is lost. Cost: rent and land prices must suit the income of the business."),
            PQ("Explain why a bank branch needs a different location from a record-storage office.", "The bank serves walk-in customers, so it needs an accessible, visible and secure site, usually in a town centre. A record-storage office is visited rarely, so it can be in a cheaper, quieter place, as long as documents are safe from theft, fire and damp."),
            SUM("An office is where the clerical and administrative work of an organization is done.",
                "Main functions: receiving, recording, storing, processing and supplying information, plus safeguarding assets and supporting other departments.",
                "Location factors: accessibility, nearness to services, cost, utilities and communication, security and surroundings, and room for growth.",
                "In answers, give several factors and a reason for each; match the factor to the type of business."),
        ],
    ),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1.2
    lesson(
        module=M1,
        topic="Office layouts",
        title="Open-plan and cellular office layouts",
        slug="ofp1-office-layouts",
        minutes=20,
        summary="Office layout is the way rooms, desks and equipment are arranged. An open-plan office puts many workers in one large room; a cellular office divides staff into separate rooms. Each has advantages and disadvantages.",
        objectives=[
            "Define office layout and say why it matters",
            "Describe open-plan and cellular (private) offices",
            "Give advantages and disadvantages of each layout",
            "Recommend a layout for a given type of work",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Layout", "Open-plan office", "Cellular office", "Privacy", "Supervision", "Partitions"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("Walk into the secretariat of a large secondary school. You may find ten typists and clerks in one big hall, with the head clerk's desk at the back. "
              "Now walk into a ministry building: a long corridor with many doors, each opening onto a small office with one or two officers. "
              "Both are offices, but they are laid out differently."),
            T("**Layout** affects how fast work flows, how well staff are supervised, how much noise there is and how much the building costs. "
              "In this lesson you will learn the two main layouts that Office Practice examinations ask about."),
            H("What is office layout?"),
            D("**Office layout** is the way furniture, equipment, workers and walls are arranged inside the office space so that work can be done smoothly, comfortably and safely.",
              title="Definition: office layout"),
            T("A good layout lets paper and people move without long walks, keeps noisy machines away from quiet workers, puts people who work together close to each other and leaves clear exits for safety."),
            IMG("f5ofp1-layouts.svg", alt="Two plan drawings side by side. On the left, an open-plan office: one large rectangle with rows of desks and a supervisor's desk at the back. On the right, a cellular office: a corridor with separate small rooms on both sides.",
                caption="Figure 1. An open-plan office (left) and a cellular office (right). Schematic plans, not to scale."),
            H("The open-plan office"),
            D("An **open-plan office** is one large room, usually without internal walls, where many workers sit together. Desks may be in rows or in groups, sometimes divided by low screens (partitions) or plants.",
              title="Definition: open-plan office"),
            KEY("Open-plan: advantages and disadvantages", "Learn both sides.", cards=[
                ["Advantages", "Easy supervision, because the supervisor sees everyone. Easy communication and teamwork. Cheaper to build, heat or cool and light. Equipment such as photocopiers can be shared. Layout is easy to change. It uses space well."],
                ["Disadvantages", "More noise and interruptions. Little privacy, so confidential work is difficult. Germs and gossip spread easily. Staff may feel watched. One loud conversation disturbs everybody."],
            ]),
            H("The cellular (private) office"),
            D("A **cellular office** is a building divided into small rooms (cells), each used by one person or a few people. The rooms usually open onto a corridor.",
              title="Definition: cellular office"),
            KEY("Cellular: advantages and disadvantages", "These are the mirror image of open-plan.", cards=[
                ["Advantages", "Privacy and quiet, so staff can concentrate. Confidential discussions and telephone calls are possible. The room can be locked to protect documents. Fewer interruptions. A senior person's status is clear."],
                ["Disadvantages", "More expensive: more walls, doors, lights and air-conditioning. Harder to supervise. Slower communication between rooms. Equipment must be duplicated or carried. Layout is hard to change."],
            ]),
            T("Many modern organizations combine the two: an open area for clerical staff, with small private rooms for managers and meetings. "
              "Some use **hot-desking**, where staff do not own a desk but take any free one, or allow part of the work to be done from home."),
            EX("Worked example 1: recommend a layout", intro="A school bursar's office with five clerks who type letters and answer routine questions all day wants to improve supervision and save money. Which layout should the bursar choose?", steps=[
                S("Identify the type of work", text="Routine, similar tasks, little confidential discussion."),
                S("Match to advantages", text="Open-plan gives easy supervision, lower cost and shared equipment, all of which the bursar wants."),
                S("Note the weakness", text="Noise: the bursar should place the printer away from the clerks and keep a small separate room for confidential matters such as pay."),
                S("Answer", text="Open-plan for the five clerks, with one private room for confidential work."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: recommend a layout", intro="A firm of accountants (invented for practice) handles customers' financial secrets. Each accountant needs to talk on the phone and meet clients. Which layout suits better?", steps=[
                S("Identify the key need", text="Confidentiality and quiet."),
                S("Match to layout", text="A cellular office gives privacy and lockable rooms."),
                S("Accept the cost", text="Rent and construction are higher, but the firm protects its clients' secrets and reputation."),
                S("Answer", text="A cellular layout."),
            ]),
            TIP("To answer \"compare\" or \"state advantages and disadvantages\" questions, write in pairs: privacy v. supervision, cost v. quiet. A pair shows the reader you understand the trade-off."),
            MISTAKE("Mixing up the two layouts", "Cellular means cells, that is, small closed rooms. Open-plan means open space. A good memory trick: open-plan = open for everybody to see."),
            MISTAKE("Saying open-plan is always better because it is cheaper", "Cost is one factor only. Where work is confidential or needs deep concentration, a cellular office may be better even at a higher cost."),
            QUIZ("Which is a disadvantage of an open-plan office?",
                 ["It is easy to supervise staff", "Noise and lack of privacy", "Equipment can be shared", "It costs less to light and heat"], 1,
                 "In one large room, sounds travel and confidential matters are hard to protect. The other options are advantages.",
                 hint="Think about 10 people in one room, all on the phone.",
                 feedback=["That is an advantage.", "", "That is an advantage.", "That is an advantage."]),
            QUIZ("A manager who often holds private interviews with staff would choose...",
                 ["a desk in the middle of an open-plan area", "a private office (cell)", "a desk beside the photocopier", "a desk near the reception door"], 1,
                 "Private interviews need quiet and confidentiality, which a closed room provides.",
                 hint="Where can the door be closed?",
                 feedback=["Everyone would overhear.", "", "Noise and passing staff would disturb.", "Visitors and noise would disturb the interview."]),
            PQ("Distinguish between an open-plan and a cellular office.", "An open-plan office is one large room where many workers sit together without separating walls. A cellular office is divided into small rooms, each for one person or a few people."),
            PQ("State three advantages and three disadvantages of an open-plan office.", "Advantages: easy supervision; cheaper to heat, light and build; easy communication and sharing of equipment. Disadvantages: noise; lack of privacy; interruptions and spread of gossip or illness."),
            PQ("A firm decides to move from cellular offices to an open-plan office. Give two reasons why it may do so and one problem it may face.", "Reasons: lower costs of space, heating and lighting; easier supervision and teamwork. Problem: noise and loss of privacy, which may reduce concentration; confidential work will need a separate room."),
            SUM("Office layout is the arrangement of furniture, equipment and people in the office space.",
                "Open-plan: one large room; cheap and easy to supervise but noisy and with little privacy.",
                "Cellular: separate small rooms; private and quiet, but dearer and harder to supervise.",
                "Choose the layout that fits the type of work and the money available; many offices combine both."),
        ],
    ),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1.3
    lesson(
        module=M1,
        topic="Ergonomics, health and safety",
        title="Ergonomics, health and safety in the office",
        slug="ofp1-ergonomics-health-and-safety",
        minutes=20,
        summary="Ergonomics fits the job and equipment to the worker. Good lighting, ventilation, seating and layout, together with fire precautions and safe habits, protect staff health and increase productivity.",
        objectives=[
            "Define ergonomics and explain why it matters",
            "Describe good lighting, ventilation, temperature, noise control and seating",
            "List common office hazards and the precautions against them",
            "Explain basic fire safety and first aid in the office",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Ergonomics", "Ventilation", "Lighting", "Posture", "Hazard", "Fire safety", "First aid", "Fatigue"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("A secretary in Yaounde types for six hours a day on a low stool at a high table, with a window behind the screen throwing glare. By the end of the week her back, neck and eyes hurt. "
              "Her work gets slower and she makes more mistakes. The problem is not her skill. It is the way her workplace has been set up."),
            T("Offices look safe, yet accidents and illnesses do happen there: falls over cables, cuts, burns, back pain and eye strain. A good office protects its staff, and healthy staff work better."),
            H("What is ergonomics?"),
            D("**Ergonomics** is the study of fitting the job, the furniture and the equipment to the worker, so that the work is comfortable, safe and efficient. "
              "It is sometimes called \"human engineering\".",
              title="Definition: ergonomics",
              note="The aim is to adapt the workplace to the person, not the person to the workplace."),
            KEY("Ergonomic features of a good office", "Know each feature and the reason for it.", cards=[
                ["Lighting", "Enough natural or electric light, without glare on screens. Poor light causes eye strain and headaches."],
                ["Ventilation and temperature", "Fresh air from windows, fans or air conditioning. A room that is too hot or stuffy makes people tired; too cold makes fingers stiff."],
                ["Noise control", "Place noisy machines like printers away from quiet workers, use partitions or soft furnishings. Noise causes stress and mistakes."],
                ["Furniture and seating", "An adjustable chair with back support, a desk at the right height, and a screen at eye level prevent back pain and strain."],
                ["Space and layout", "Enough room to move, clear gangways, and frequently used items within reach."],
                ["Rest and breaks", "Short breaks away from the screen, and changing posture, reduce tiredness (fatigue)."],
            ]),
            H("Health and safety in the office"),
            D("A **hazard** is anything that can cause harm. **Health and safety** is the set of rules and habits that protect workers from accidents and illness at work.",
              title="Definition: hazard; health and safety"),
            T("The table below pairs common hazards with sensible precautions. It is a CODE block so the columns line up on a phone."),
            CODE("Hazard                        Precaution\n"
                 "----------------------------  -----------------------------------------\n"
                 "Trailing cables               Tape them down or route them along walls\n"
                 "Overloaded power sockets      Use one plug per socket; check wiring\n"
                 "Wet floors                    Put up a warning sign; dry the floor\n"
                 "Open drawers, loose carpets   Close drawers; repair or remove carpets\n"
                 "Heavy boxes of files          Lift with bent knees; use a trolley\n"
                 "Sharp tools (scissors, pins)  Store safely; use guards\n"
                 "Long hours at a screen        Breaks; adjustable chair; correct light\n"
                 "Fire                          Alarms, extinguishers, clear exits",
                 "text", title="Hazards and precautions"),
            T("**Fire safety** matters especially because offices hold paper, which burns easily. Good practice includes: fire extinguishers of the right type and staff trained to use them; "
              "smoke alarms and a clear alarm signal; exits kept unlocked and free of boxes; a plan showing exits; and regular fire drills so that everyone knows where to go. "
              "Do not use water on an electrical fire. Check the local rules and your textbook for the types of extinguisher."),
            T("**First aid** means the first help given to an injured or ill person before a doctor or ambulance arrives. An office should have a first-aid box with clean dressings, gloves and antiseptic, a person trained to use it, and a list of emergency numbers. Check the list of contents with your teacher."),
            EX("Worked example 1: spot the hazards", intro="A visitor notices these things in a small office: a power strip with five machines plugged in, a box of files blocking the exit, and a clerk's screen facing a bright window. State the problem with each and the correction.", steps=[
                S("Overloaded power strip", text="Risk of overheating and fire. Spread the load, unplug unused machines, and have the wiring checked."),
                S("Blocked exit", text="People cannot escape in an emergency. Move the box and keep the exit clear."),
                S("Screen facing a window", text="Glare causes eye strain. Turn the screen sideways to the window or use blinds."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: ergonomic improvement", intro="Typists in a school office complain of backache. Suggest four changes.", steps=[
                S("Seating", text="Provide chairs with back support and adjustable height."),
                S("Desk and screen", text="Set the keyboard at elbow height and the screen at eye level."),
                S("Breaks", text="Allow a short break each hour to stand and stretch."),
                S("Lighting", text="Add a lamp or move desks so light falls on the page without glare."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Thinking ergonomics is only about chairs", "Seating is one part. Ergonomics also covers lighting, noise, temperature, layout, equipment and rest breaks. Always mention more than one."),
            MISTAKE("Using water on an electrical fire", "Water conducts electricity and may cause shock. Switch off the power if it is safe, and use the correct extinguisher. Learn the types and check local rules."),
            TIP("In an essay on health and safety, link every hazard to a precaution and to a result: for example, \"cables on the floor cause falls, so they should be fixed along the wall to prevent injuries and lost working time\"."),
            QUIZ("Ergonomics is best defined as...",
                 ["a type of office machine", "fitting the job and equipment to the worker", "a method of filing", "a kind of insurance"], 1,
                 "Ergonomics adapts the workplace to the person for comfort, safety and efficiency.",
                 hint="The word comes from Greek words for work and law/rules.",
                 feedback=["It is not a machine but a way of designing the workplace.", "", "Filing is a separate topic.", "Insurance is a separate financial arrangement."]),
            QUIZ("Which action best prevents falls in an office?",
                 ["Leaving cables across the floor", "Fixing cables along the wall", "Keeping drawers open", "Piling boxes in the corridor"], 1,
                 "A tidy route with cables out of the way prevents tripping.",
                 hint="Which choice removes an obstacle from where people walk?",
                 feedback=["People can trip on cables.", "", "Open drawers can be hit or tripped on.", "Boxes block the path."]),
            PQ("Define ergonomics and list four features of an ergonomically designed office.", "Ergonomics is the study of fitting the job, furniture and equipment to the worker. Features: adequate lighting, good ventilation and temperature, noise control, adjustable seating and desks at the right height, enough space, and rest breaks."),
            PQ("State four precautions an office should take against fire.", "Install smoke alarms; provide extinguishers and train staff; keep exits free and unlocked; hold fire drills; avoid overloading sockets; store waste paper away from heat. Any four."),
            PQ("Explain why an employer should care about the health and safety of office staff.", "Healthy staff are absent less and work faster with fewer errors. Accidents cost time and money in compensation and repairs. A safe workplace keeps morale high and may be required by law. (Check the current labour rules.)"),
            SUM("Ergonomics fits the job, furniture and equipment to the worker.",
                "Good lighting, ventilation, noise control, seating and rest breaks reduce tiredness and strain.",
                "Hazards such as cables, overloaded sockets, wet floors and heavy loads need simple precautions.",
                "Fire safety (alarms, extinguishers, clear exits, drills) and first aid are part of every office's plan."),
        ],
    ),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1.4
    lesson(
        module=M1,
        topic="Organizational charts",
        title="Organizational charts: line, functional and line-and-staff",
        slug="ofp1-organizational-charts",
        minutes=22,
        summary="An organizational chart is a diagram of the posts in an organization and who reports to whom. Line organization has one chain of command, functional organization uses specialist supervisors, and line-and-staff adds advisers to the line.",
        objectives=[
            "Define an organizational chart and state its uses",
            "Read a chart: levels, lines of authority, span of control",
            "Describe line, functional and line-and-staff structures",
            "Give advantages and disadvantages of each structure",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Organizational chart", "Chain of command", "Authority", "Span of control", "Line organization", "Functional organization", "Line-and-staff", "Delegation"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("In a school, the principal gives instructions to vice-principals, who instruct heads of department, who instruct teachers. Everyone knows whom to report to. "
              "A new teacher can learn this on the first day because it is drawn on a chart in the staff room."),
            T("A business or ministry is bigger and more complicated, so it draws such a chart to show how it is organized. Reading and drawing these charts is a common examination skill."),
            H("What is an organizational chart?"),
            D("An **organizational chart** (organogram) is a diagram that shows the posts in an organization, how they are grouped into departments, and who reports to whom.",
              title="Definition: organizational chart",
              note="In the chart, boxes are posts and lines are lines of authority or reporting."),
            KEY("Uses of an organizational chart", "Why organizations draw one.", cards=[
                ["Clarity", "Staff see who their boss is and who works under them."],
                ["Orientation", "New staff see where they fit and whom to ask."],
                ["Planning", "Management can spot gaps, overlaps or too many layers."],
                ["Communication and control", "Orders and reports follow clear lines. Each person is accountable for their area."],
            ]),
            T("Some words you need. **Authority** is the right to give orders and make decisions. The **chain of command** is the line of authority from the top down. "
              "**Span of control** is the number of subordinates who report directly to one superior. **Delegation** is handing part of your authority and tasks to someone below you while you remain responsible."),
            H("Line organization"),
            D("In a **line organization** authority flows in a single straight line from the top to the bottom. Each person has only one superior and gives orders only to people directly below.",
              title="Definition: line organization"),
            IMG("f5ofp1-org-line.svg", alt="Organizational chart: a Managing Director at the top, three managers (production, sales, accounts) below, and two sections under each manager. Solid lines join each post to the one above.",
                caption="Figure 1. A line organization. Post names are examples only."),
            KEY("Line: advantages and disadvantages", "A simple structure.", cards=[
                ["Advantages", "Clear authority, simple, quick decisions, easy discipline, each person knows their one boss. Suitable for small firms."],
                ["Disadvantages", "Heavy load on managers, who must know a lot. Slow communication in a big firm, because messages pass through every level. Little room for specialist advice."],
            ]),
            H("Functional organization"),
            D("In a **functional organization** work is divided by function or specialty (for example finance, personnel, production). Specialists supervise all workers in their area of expertise, so a worker may take orders from more than one specialist.",
              title="Definition: functional organization"),
            T("For example, a clerk might be told by the accountant how to keep the cash book and by the personnel manager how to complete leave forms. "
              "Specialization improves the quality of advice, but the worker can receive conflicting instructions, which may cause confusion about who the real boss is."),
            H("Line-and-staff organization"),
            D("A **line-and-staff organization** keeps the line of command but adds **staff** posts: advisers and specialists who give advice and services to line managers but do not command the line.",
              title="Definition: line-and-staff organization"),
            IMG("f5ofp1-org-line-staff.svg", alt="Organizational chart: a Managing Director with two line managers below, each with two sections. A Legal Adviser box to the right is joined to the Managing Director by a dashed line, showing advice only.",
                caption="Figure 2. A line-and-staff organization. The dashed line shows advice, not command. Names invented for the diagram."),
            T("Typical staff posts are a legal adviser, a personnel adviser or an IT specialist. The line managers may follow or ignore the advice, and the decision remains theirs."),
            KEY("Line-and-staff: advantages and disadvantages", "A mix of the first two.", cards=[
                ["Advantages", "Combines clear command with expert advice. Reduces the managers' workload. Better decisions."],
                ["Disadvantages", "More posts, so more cost. Conflict can arise if advisers think they should command. Advice can be slow or ignored."],
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 1: read a chart", intro="Look at Figure 1. How many managers report to the Managing Director? To whom do the sections report? What is the span of control of the Managing Director?", steps=[
                S("Count the boxes on the second level", text="Three managers report directly to the Managing Director."),
                S("Follow the lines under a manager", text="Each manager has two sections reporting to him or her."),
                S("Span of control", text="The Managing Director's span of control is 3, because three people report directly. Each manager's span is 2."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: choose a structure", intro="A small family workshop with eight workers and one owner (invented) is growing. Which structure suits it now?", steps=[
                S("Size", text="Small, with simple tasks."),
                S("Match", text="A line organization is simple, cheap and gives quick decisions."),
                S("Later", text="If it grows, the owner can add staff posts, such as an accountant, giving a line-and-staff structure."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Counting everybody as the span of control", "Span of control counts only the people who report DIRECTLY to a superior. Sections under a manager belong to the manager's span, not to the director's."),
            MISTAKE("Saying staff advisers give orders to line workers", "Staff positions advise. The authority to command stays in the line. Always draw advice with a dashed line to show the difference."),
            QUIZ("In a line organization each person has...",
                 ["many bosses", "one superior", "no superior", "only advisers above"], 1,
                 "Line authority is a single chain, so each person reports to one superior.",
                 hint="The word \"line\" suggests one straight line.",
                 feedback=["That describes confusion or a functional system.", "", "Everyone except the owner has a superior.", "Advisers do not command."]),
            QUIZ("A legal adviser who advises the Managing Director but cannot command production staff holds a...",
                 ["line post", "staff post", "customer post", "temporary post only"], 1,
                 "A post that gives advice or service without command authority is a staff post.",
                 hint="Does the adviser give orders to the line?",
                 feedback=["Line posts carry command authority.", "", "Customers are outside the organization.", "Staff posts can be permanent."]),
            PQ("Define an organizational chart and state three of its uses.", "An organizational chart is a diagram showing the posts in an organization and the lines of authority between them. Uses: shows who reports to whom, helps new staff understand where they fit, helps management spot gaps and overlaps, and gives clear lines for communication and control."),
            PQ("Distinguish between line organization and line-and-staff organization.", "In a line organization, authority runs in one straight chain from the top to the bottom. A line-and-staff organization keeps this chain but adds staff posts of specialists who advise line managers without commanding them."),
            PQ("State two advantages and two disadvantages of a line organization.", "Advantages: simple and easy to understand; clear authority and quick decisions. Disadvantages: managers carry a heavy workload; the organization is slow to communicate when big, and has little specialist advice."),
            SUM("An organizational chart shows posts and lines of authority.",
                "Line: one chain of command, one boss each; simple but heavy on managers.",
                "Functional: specialists supervise by function; expert but may give conflicting orders.",
                "Line-and-staff: line command plus advisers; better advice but more cost and possible conflict.",
                "Span of control counts only direct subordinates."),
        ],
    ),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1.5
    lesson(
        module=M1,
        topic="Duties of office personnel",
        title="Who does what in the office: duties of office personnel",
        slug="ofp1-duties-of-office-personnel",
        minutes=20,
        summary="Office staff have different posts: office manager, secretary, receptionist, typist, clerks, filing clerk, messenger and others. Each has defined duties, and good staff also share qualities such as accuracy, honesty and discretion.",
        objectives=[
            "Name the main posts in an office and state their duties",
            "Distinguish between a secretary, a clerk and a typist",
            "State the qualities needed by office staff",
            "Match a task to the member of staff who should do it",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Office manager", "Secretary", "Clerk", "Typist", "Filing clerk", "Messenger", "Confidentiality", "Job description"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("In a busy district hospital administration office, a letter arrives for the director. A clerk opens and records it. The secretary reads it and puts it in the director's folder with the earlier correspondence. "
              "The director dictates a reply. A typist types it. A messenger carries it to the ministry. Each person did a different part of the job."),
            T("Work is shared in this way so that tasks are done by the people best trained for them and nothing is forgotten. The list of duties for each post is its **job description**."),
            H("The main posts and their duties"),
            D("A **job description** is a written list of the duties, responsibilities and reporting line of a post. It helps an employee know what is expected and helps the employer decide whom to hire or promote.",
              title="Definition: job description"),
            KEY("Office manager and secretary", "Titles differ between organizations, so learn the duties, not just the name.", cards=[
                ["Office manager (or administrative officer)", "Plans and supervises the work of the office, recruits and trains staff, buys equipment and stationery, controls the office budget, makes sure procedures and safety rules are followed, and reports to senior management."],
                ["Secretary / personal assistant", "Handles correspondence, keeps the diary of the boss, arranges meetings and travel, drafts letters, takes notes and prepares minutes, deals with confidential matters, files documents and screens calls and visitors."],
            ]),
            KEY("Clerks, typists and others", "Learn at least one duty for each.", cards=[
                ["Receptionist", "Greets and directs visitors, answers calls, keeps the visitors' register (next lesson)."],
                ["Typist / word-processing operator", "Types letters, reports and tables from handwritten drafts, dictation or recordings; checks spelling and layout."],
                ["General clerk", "Records data, writes simple letters, checks documents, copies, answers routine queries and helps wherever needed."],
                ["Filing clerk (records clerk)", "Sorts, files and retrieves documents, keeps the index and removes old files for storage or destruction."],
                ["Mail clerk", "Receives, sorts, opens, records and dispatches mail (Module 3)."],
                ["Cashier", "Receives and pays cash and cheques, issues receipts, and balances the cash book."],
                ["Messenger / office assistant", "Carries documents and messages inside and outside the building, runs errands, helps with cleaning and setting up the meeting room."],
                ["Data-entry / IT officer", "Enters records in the computer, keeps backups and helps staff with computers and printers."],
            ]),
            T("In a small business, one person may do several of these jobs. In a large one, every task has its own post."),
            H("Qualities of good office staff"),
            T("Technical skills are not enough. Office staff also need **accuracy** (few errors), **honesty**, **punctuality**, **discretion** (keeping private business private), politeness, "
              "the ability to work with others, willingness to learn new methods, neat appearance and good communication in speech and writing."),
            D("**Confidentiality** means keeping private information, such as salaries, customers' accounts or the boss's plans, secret and sharing it only with people who are allowed to know.",
              title="Definition: confidentiality"),
            EX("Worked example 1: who should do the task?", intro="Match each task to the best member of staff: (a) type a 3-page report; (b) take a parcel of documents to the council; (c) find last year's invoice from a supplier; (d) arrange the manager's travel for next week.", steps=[
                S("(a) Typing a report", text="Typist (or word-processing operator)."),
                S("(b) Carrying documents outside", text="Messenger."),
                S("(c) Finding an old invoice", text="Filing clerk (records clerk)."),
                S("(d) Arranging the manager's travel", text="Secretary or personal assistant."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: explain a difference", intro="Distinguish between a secretary and a typist.", steps=[
                S("Secretary", text="A broader, higher-level post: manages the boss's diary and correspondence, arranges meetings, handles confidential matters and often supervises junior staff."),
                S("Typist", text="A narrower post: produces typed documents from drafts or dictation."),
                S("Link", text="A secretary may type, but a typist does not usually manage the boss's work."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Listing only qualities, not duties", "A question that asks for duties needs actions (\"types letters\", \"keeps the diary\"). Qualities such as honesty belong in a separate answer."),
            MISTAKE("Using the same duty for every post", "Do not write \"files documents\" for every clerk. Pick duties that clearly belong to that post and are different from the others."),
            TIP("Duties of staff can also appear in Paper 2 case-study questions. Read the case, find the weak point (for example, \"the secretary shared the salary list\") and name the quality or procedure that was missing."),
            QUIZ("Who is mainly responsible for finding old documents in the filing cabinet?",
                 ["Typist", "Filing clerk", "Cashier", "Messenger"], 1,
                 "A filing clerk (records clerk) sorts, files and retrieves documents.",
                 hint="Which post has the word \"filing\" in it?",
                 feedback=["A typist produces typed documents.", "", "A cashier handles money.", "A messenger carries items between places."]),
            QUIZ("Keeping the contents of the boss's letters private is called...",
                 ["punctuality", "confidentiality", "delegation", "dictation"], 1,
                 "Confidentiality means not sharing private information with people who are not allowed to know it.",
                 hint="Which word means \"keep it secret\"?",
                 feedback=["Punctuality is arriving on time.", "", "Delegation is passing tasks to others.", "Dictation is speaking a text for someone to write."]),
            PQ("State four duties of a secretary.", "Handling correspondence; keeping the boss's diary; arranging meetings and travel; taking notes and preparing minutes; screening calls and visitors; filing documents. Any four."),
            PQ("List four qualities of a good office worker and explain one.", "Accuracy, honesty, punctuality, discretion, politeness, willingness to learn. Discretion: an office worker sees private information such as salaries and must not share it outside, otherwise people lose trust in the organization."),
            PQ("A new clerk repeatedly makes mistakes on invoices. As office manager, state two actions you would take.", "Check the work and show the clerk the errors; provide training or a checklist; supervise for a period; give feedback and review progress later. Keep the discussion private and polite."),
            SUM("Each post has its own duties, written in a job description.",
                "Secretary: correspondence, diary, meetings, confidential work. Typist: typing. Clerk: records and routine work. Filing clerk: stores and finds documents. Messenger: carries items.",
                "Office manager plans and supervises the office as a whole.",
                "Good staff are accurate, honest, punctual, discreet, polite and ready to learn."),
        ],
    ),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2.1
    lesson(
        module=M2,
        topic="The receptionist",
        title="The receptionist: qualities and duties",
        slug="ofp1-the-receptionist",
        minutes=18,
        summary="The receptionist is the first person visitors meet and speak to, so he or she represents the whole organization. Good receptionists are polite, well groomed, tactful and well organized, and they greet, direct, record and protect.",
        objectives=[
            "Explain why the receptionist's role is important",
            "State the qualities of a good receptionist",
            "List the duties of a receptionist",
            "Handle common difficult situations politely",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Receptionist", "First impression", "Tact", "Courtesy", "Discretion", "Screening", "Reception area"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("When you go to a hospital, a bank or a government office, the first person you speak to may decide your whole opinion of the place. "
              "If the receptionist smiles, listens and points you to the right desk, you feel welcome. If you are ignored or sent to the wrong door, you may leave annoyed, even if the rest of the organization is excellent."),
            T("The receptionist is the \"face\" of the organization. That is why this post needs careful selection and training."),
            H("What a receptionist does"),
            D("A **receptionist** is the member of staff who receives visitors and telephone calls at the entrance or reception area of an organization, gives them the right information and directs them to the right person.",
              title="Definition: receptionist"),
            KEY("Duties of a receptionist", "Learn these with an example for each.", cards=[
                ["Welcome and greet", "Greet every visitor politely and promptly, in the language the visitor prefers where possible."],
                ["Find out the purpose", "Ask who the visitor wishes to see and why, politely, so that the right person is informed."],
                ["Check appointments and inform staff", "Look at the appointment diary, telephone or message the staff member, and ask the visitor to wait."],
                ["Record visitors", "Enter name, company, time in, person visited and time out in the visitors' register (next lesson), and issue a visitor's pass if the organization uses one."],
                ["Direct and escort", "Give clear directions or take the visitor to the office. Offer a seat and perhaps water or reading material."],
                ["Handle calls and messages", "Answer the main telephone or switchboard, take accurate messages and pass them on."],
                ["Protect and screen", "Keep unwanted callers away from busy managers politely, and notice anything unsafe or suspicious."],
                ["Keep the reception area tidy", "Tidy seats and magazines, display notices and keep the area clean and welcoming."],
            ]),
            H("Qualities of a good receptionist"),
            KEY("Personal qualities", "These are often asked as a list with a reason for each.", cards=[
                ["Courtesy and a pleasant manner", "Polite, smiling, patient, even with rude visitors."],
                ["Good appearance", "Clean, neat dress that suits the organization, because first impressions matter."],
                ["Tact", "Saying the right thing in a way that does not offend, for example, \"Mr Njoh is in a meeting, may I take a message?\" rather than \"He doesn't want to see you.\""],
                ["Good speech and listening", "Speaks clearly, listens carefully, and does not interrupt."],
                ["Memory for names and faces", "Calling regular visitors by name makes them feel valued."],
                ["Discretion and honesty", "Does not repeat confidential information heard at the desk."],
                ["Organization and calm", "Can deal with several visitors and calls at once without panic."],
                ["Knowledge of the organization", "Knows the departments, staff, services and procedures."],
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 1: a visitor without an appointment", intro="A visitor arrives and asks to see the director immediately. There is no appointment in the diary. What should the receptionist do?", steps=[
                S("Greet politely", text="\"Good morning, how may I help you?\""),
                S("Find the purpose", text="Ask the visitor's name and what the matter is about."),
                S("Check availability", text="Telephone the director's secretary and give the name and purpose."),
                S("Act on the answer", text="If the director cannot see the visitor, offer an appointment for another time or direct the visitor to a suitable officer. Be polite, and never promise what you cannot give."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: a rude visitor", intro="A visitor shouts that he has waited too long. How should the receptionist react?", steps=[
                S("Stay calm and polite", text="Do not shout back. Lower your voice and show you are listening."),
                S("Apologize and explain", text="\"I am sorry for the wait. Mr Tabi is finishing an urgent meeting.\""),
                S("Offer help", text="Offer a seat, a message or a new time."),
                S("Report if needed", text="If he becomes threatening, call a supervisor or security."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Promising an appointment you cannot arrange", "A receptionist who says \"The director will see you now\" without checking creates embarrassment. Check first, then answer."),
            MISTAKE("Chatting on a phone while a visitor waits", "Acknowledge the visitor with a nod or a short \"I will be with you in a moment\". Ignoring people suggests that the organization does not respect its clients."),
            TIP("Where the question asks for the duties of a receptionist, give at least six, including recording the visitors and direct or escort. Where it asks for qualities, add a reason for each."),
            QUIZ("Why is a receptionist's appearance and manner important?",
                 ["Because visitors form their first impression of the organization from the receptionist", "Because receptionists never leave the desk", "Because dress has no effect on staff", "Because visitors do not need help"], 0,
                 "The receptionist is the first contact, and first impressions shape how people view the whole organization.",
                 hint="Who do visitors meet first?",
                 feedback=["", "This is not a reason for good appearance.", "Dress and manner do affect how an organization is seen.", "Visitors need directions and help, which is the receptionist's job."]),
            QUIZ("Tact means...",
                 ["speaking so as not to offend", "speaking loudly", "avoiding visitors", "refusing every request"], 0,
                 "Tact is the ability to say difficult things without hurting or offending the listener.",
                 hint="Think of the polite way to say no.",
                 feedback=["", "Loudness is not tact.", "Avoiding visitors is the opposite of the job.", "Refusing everything is not tactful, just unhelpful."]),
            PQ("State six duties of a receptionist.", "Greeting visitors; finding out their purpose; checking appointments; informing staff; recording visitors in the register; directing or escorting visitors; answering calls and taking messages; keeping the reception area tidy. Any six."),
            PQ("Explain four qualities of a good receptionist.", "Courtesy: polite to all visitors. Tact: avoids offending. Good appearance: creates a good first impression. Discretion: does not repeat confidential information. Also memory, calmness, clear speech."),
            PQ("A visitor wants to see the manager, who is busy. Describe how the receptionist should handle the situation.", "Greet, ask the visitor's name and purpose; tell the manager or secretary; if the manager is busy, politely tell the visitor and offer a seat, a later appointment or a message; stay courteous and do not promise anything unchecked."),
            SUM("The receptionist is the first contact, so he or she represents the whole organization.",
                "Duties: greet, find purpose, check appointments, inform staff, record visitors, direct, handle calls, keep the area tidy.",
                "Qualities: courtesy, good appearance, tact, clear speech, memory, discretion, calm organization.",
                "Handle difficult visitors politely and escalate threats to a supervisor."),
        ],
    ),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2.2
    lesson(
        module=M2,
        topic="Visitors and appointments",
        title="Receiving visitors, the visitors' register and the appointment diary",
        slug="ofp1-visitors-and-appointments",
        minutes=22,
        summary="Visitors are received politely, their details recorded in a visitors' register, and the manager's time is planned through an appointment diary. Both records protect security and avoid double-booking.",
        objectives=[
            "Describe the steps in receiving a visitor with and without an appointment",
            "Say why a visitors' register is kept and what it contains",
            "Make and change entries in an appointment diary",
            "Explain how to avoid clashes between appointments",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Visitor", "Appointment", "Visitors' register", "Visitor's pass", "Appointment diary", "Double-booking", "Screening"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("A ministry official receives dozens of callers in a week: a supplier, a student with a request, a delegation from another town. "
              "If every caller walked in at any time, the official would never finish any work. Organizations therefore manage visits with two simple tools: a register and a diary."),
            H("Receiving visitors step by step"),
            KEY("The usual procedure", "Learn the order of the steps.", cards=[
                ["1. Greet", "Stand or look up, smile and greet the visitor politely."],
                ["2. Ask", "Ask the visitor's name, organization and who they wish to see, and the purpose of the visit."],
                ["3. Check", "Look in the appointment diary. If there is an appointment, confirm it. If not, call the person to ask if they can see the visitor."],
                ["4. Record", "Write the details in the visitors' register. Issue a visitor's pass if the organization uses them."],
                ["5. Inform and direct", "Tell the person that the visitor is here, then show or escort the visitor, or ask the visitor to wait in a seat."],
                ["6. Sign out", "When the visitor leaves, note the time out and take back the pass."],
            ]),
            H("The visitors' register"),
            D("A **visitors' register** (visitors' book) is a record of everyone who enters the organization as a visitor. It is kept at reception.",
              title="Definition: visitors' register"),
            T("It is kept for three main reasons: **security** (the organization knows who is in the building, especially in an emergency such as a fire), "
              "**reference** (to find out later who called and when) and **control** (staff are not disturbed by unknown people). Typical columns are shown below. The names are invented for practice."),
            CODE("Date      Name            Organization   Person visited  Purpose        In     Out    Pass\n"
                 "--------  --------------  -------------  --------------  -------------  -----  -----  ----\n"
                 "12 Mar    A. Fon          Fon Supplies   Bursar          Quotation      09:05  09:40  07\n"
                 "12 Mar    B. Mbarga       Parent         Principal       School fees    09:20  10:10  08\n"
                 "12 Mar    C. Ndifor       Repairs firm   Admin officer   Printer fix    10:15  11:30  09",
                 "text", title="Visitors' register (invented entries)"),
            T("A **visitor's pass** (badge) lets staff recognise that a person has been registered. It is returned when leaving. Some organizations also check an identity card."),
            H("The appointment diary"),
            D("An **appointment** is an agreed time for a meeting. An **appointment diary** is a book or computer calendar in which the time, name and purpose of each appointment are written, so that the manager's time is planned and clashes are avoided.",
              title="Definition: appointment diary"),
            T("Appointments can be made by telephone, letter, email, message (for example WhatsApp) or in person. The person who makes the entry should note: date, time, name of visitor, organization, telephone number, purpose and expected length. "
              "When plans change, cross out in pencil or mark \"cancelled\" and write the new entry; do not simply rub out."),
            CODE("MONDAY 14 APRIL (Manager: Mr X - invented)\n"
                 "08:30  Staff briefing (office)\n"
                 "10:00  Mrs Ayuk, Ayuk Traders - contract (30 min) tel 6xx xxx xxx\n"
                 "11:00  Inspector from the council - visit (1 hour)\n"
                 "13:00  LUNCH\n"
                 "14:30  Phone call to head office\n"
                 "16:00  FREE",
                 "text", title="A page from an appointment diary (invented)"),
            EX("Worked example 1: making an appointment", intro="Mrs Okon (invented) phones to ask for a 30-minute appointment with the manager on Monday 14 April. Use the diary above to suggest a time.", steps=[
                S("Look at the booked times", text="Booked: 10:00 to 10:30 (Mrs Ayuk), 11:00 to 12:00 (inspector), 13:00 lunch, 14:30 phone call. The page lists 16:00 as free."),
                S("Check the length needed", text="30 minutes. The gap from 12:00 to 13:00 is free, and so is the time from 16:00."),
                S("Offer a slot", text="Offer 12:00 to 12:30 or 16:00 to 16:30, whichever the manager prefers."),
                S("Record and confirm", text="Write the entry in the diary and repeat the date and time to the caller. Note a telephone number in case of changes."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: a clash", intro="The manager must now attend an urgent meeting at 11:00. The inspector is booked for 11:00. What should the secretary do?", steps=[
                S("Do not ignore the clash", text="Two events cannot take place at the same time (double-booking)."),
                S("Contact the inspector", text="Explain politely and offer a new time, for example 14:30 (check the diary)."),
                S("Update the diary", text="Mark the old entry as moved, write the new time, and tell the manager."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Taking an appointment without a note of the telephone number", "If the appointment must be changed, you cannot reach the visitor. Always write a contact number."),
            MISTAKE("Forgetting to record the time out", "The register is also a safety record. If there is a fire, staff must know who is still in the building."),
            TIP("Do not fill the diary completely. Leave time for travel, for urgent matters and for the manager to think. Many offices leave a buffer of 10 or 15 minutes."),
            QUIZ("One main reason for keeping a visitors' register is...",
                 ["to know who is in the building and who called", "to list suppliers' prices", "to record staff salaries", "to control stock"], 0,
                 "The register supports security and reference. It shows who entered, when and whom they visited.",
                 hint="Think about a fire alarm: why would you need a list of people inside?",
                 feedback=["", "Prices are in price lists or quotations.", "Salaries are in payroll records.", "Stock is in stock records."]),
            QUIZ("Double-booking means...",
                 ["booking two people for the same time with one manager", "booking a hotel twice", "recording a visitor twice", "writing the diary in two colours"], 0,
                 "Double-booking creates a clash because one person cannot be in two places. A diary is kept to prevent it.",
                 hint="Think about two names in the same time slot.",
                 feedback=["", "That is a hotel booking error, not the office meaning.", "That would be a duplicate entry.", "Colours are not the point."]),
            PQ("State four items of information normally recorded in a visitors' register.", "Date; visitor's name; organization or address; person visited; purpose of the visit; time in; time out; visitor's pass number. Any four."),
            PQ("Explain why an organization keeps a visitors' register.", "For security: it shows who is in the building, helps in emergencies and discourages unauthorized people. For reference: it shows who called, when and why. For control: it helps staff avoid unplanned interruptions."),
            PQ("A caller asks for an appointment but the manager is fully booked all day. How should the secretary respond?", "Apologize, offer the nearest free time on another day, note the caller's name, number and purpose, and write the new appointment in the diary. Offer to pass on an urgent message or refer the caller to another officer if suitable."),
            SUM("Receive visitors by greeting, asking, checking, recording, informing and directing.",
                "The visitors' register records who entered and when; it supports security and reference.",
                "The appointment diary plans the manager's time and prevents double-booking.",
                "Always record contact details and update the diary when plans change."),
        ],
    ),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2.3
    lesson(
        module=M2,
        topic="Telephones and switchboards",
        title="Using the telephone and the switchboard",
        slug="ofp1-telephones-and-switchboards",
        minutes=22,
        summary="The telephone is the most used link between an organization and the outside world. Good telephone technique, accurate messages and a switchboard (PABX) that connects internal extensions to outside lines make communication fast and professional.",
        objectives=[
            "Describe good telephone technique for answering and making calls",
            "Take an accurate telephone message",
            "Explain what a switchboard (PABX), an extension and an intercom are",
            "State advantages and disadvantages of the telephone in business",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Telephone", "Switchboard", "PABX", "Extension", "Intercom", "Voicemail", "Telephone message", "Call screening"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("A customer phones a supplier to ask about a late delivery. The phone rings seven times, someone answers with \"Yes?\" and then puts the customer on hold for two minutes. "
              "The customer decides to try another supplier. A phone call can win or lose business in under a minute."),
            T("In this lesson you learn how to handle calls well, how a switchboard connects them inside an organization, and what to write on a message."),
            H("Good telephone technique"),
            KEY("Answering a call", "Follow this pattern.", cards=[
                ["Answer promptly", "Answer within a few rings. Have a pen and message pad ready."],
                ["Identify yourself", "Give a greeting, the name of the organization or department, and your name: \"Good morning, Mbah Hardware, Ngwa speaking. How may I help you?\" (names invented)"],
                ["Listen and note", "Let the caller finish. Write the name, number and purpose. Check spellings by repeating them."],
                ["Speak clearly and politely", "Moderate speed, a friendly tone, no slang or chewing. Never shout."],
                ["Transfer or take a message", "If the person is busy, say so politely and offer a message. Before you put someone on hold, ask permission and return often."],
                ["End well", "Repeat the key facts, thank the caller and let the caller put the phone down first where possible."],
            ]),
            T("When **making** a call, plan first: note the number, the points to raise and the documents you need. Introduce yourself, say why you are calling, keep the call short and thank the person. "
              "Remember that an international or long call may cost money. In many organizations private calls on the office phone are not allowed."),
            T("**Call screening** means finding out who is calling and why before putting the call through, so that the manager's time is protected. Do it politely: \"May I say who is calling, please?\""),
            H("Taking a telephone message"),
            T("Never rely on memory. Write the message on a message form, so that nothing is lost when the person returns. A good message form has the following parts."),
            CODE("TELEPHONE MESSAGE\n"
                 "For:        Mr Njoh (Manager)\n"
                 "Date/Time:  Tuesday 6 May, 10:25\n"
                 "Caller:     Mrs Fomba, Fomba Stationery (invented)\n"
                 "Number:     6xx xxx xxx\n"
                 "Message:    Order 24 is ready for collection.\n"
                 "Action:     Please call back before 12:00\n"
                 "Taken by:   Ngwa",
                 "text", title="A telephone message form (invented example)"),
            H("The switchboard"),
            D("A **switchboard** is a central telephone system that links all the telephones in an organization (the **extensions**) to the outside lines. Modern systems are called **PABX** (private automatic branch exchange). The person who operates it is the **telephonist**, or **switchboard operator**.",
              title="Definition: switchboard / PABX",
              note="An extension is one internal telephone with its own short number."),
            KEY("What a switchboard allows", "Main uses.", cards=[
                ["Incoming calls", "The operator or an automatic menu connects outside callers to the right extension."],
                ["Internal calls (intercom)", "Staff dial each other's extensions without using an outside line and without cost."],
                ["Outgoing calls", "Staff dial a code to reach an outside line."],
                ["Extra features", "Call transfer, conference calls, voicemail, call recording and call-forwarding."],
            ]),
            T("Small businesses often skip a switchboard and use mobile phones or a few shared lines. The same ideas apply: answer well, note messages, keep the numbers of customers safe."),
            H("Advantages and disadvantages of the telephone"),
            KEY("Telephone in business", "Weigh both sides.", cards=[
                ["Advantages", "Immediate two-way communication. Personal and friendly. Questions can be answered at once. Saves travelling. Mobile phones work anywhere with a network."],
                ["Disadvantages", "No written record unless notes are made. Misunderstandings from poor lines or accents. Cost of long calls. Caller may interrupt work. Network and power failures. Confidentiality can be lost if overheard."],
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 1: taking a message", intro="The manager is out. Mrs Fomba (invented) calls and asks that he phone her before noon about order 24 that is ready. Her number is 6xx xxx xxx. It is Tuesday 6 May, 10:25. Fill in the message form.", steps=[
                S("Fill in the heading", text="For: the manager. Date/time: Tuesday 6 May, 10:25."),
                S("Caller and number", text="Mrs Fomba, Fomba Stationery. Number as given. Repeat it to confirm."),
                S("Message and action", text="Message: order 24 is ready. Action: call back before 12:00."),
                S("Sign", text="Write your name under Taken by, and place the form where the manager will see it."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: a poor call", intro="A clerk answers: \"Yeah? He is not here. Call later.\" Rewrite the answer politely.", steps=[
                S("Start with a greeting", text="\"Good morning, Mbah Hardware, Ngwa speaking.\""),
                S("Say what is happening", text="\"I am sorry, Mr Njoh is out of the office at the moment.\""),
                S("Offer help", text="\"May I take a message, or would you like to call back after two o'clock?\""),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Writing messages on loose scraps", "Scraps get lost. Use a message pad or form and put it where the person will see it. Write the date, time and caller's number every time."),
            MISTAKE("Giving out confidential information over the phone", "Do not give personal or business details to a caller you cannot identify. Take a number and ask a responsible person to call back."),
            QUIZ("A person who operates the switchboard is called a...",
                 ["telephonist", "treasurer", "typist", "tutor"], 0,
                 "The telephonist (switchboard operator) connects calls.",
                 hint="Which word begins like \"telephone\"?",
                 feedback=["", "A treasurer looks after money.", "A typist produces typed documents.", "A tutor teaches."]),
            QUIZ("Which is NOT part of a good telephone message?",
                 ["The caller's name", "The caller's number", "The date and time", "The receptionist's opinion of the caller"], 3,
                 "A message records facts, not opinions.",
                 hint="Messages must be factual and useful to the reader.",
                 feedback=["Names are essential.", "The number is needed to return the call.", "Date and time show how urgent it is.", ""]),
            PQ("State five rules for answering telephone calls in an office.", "Answer promptly; give a greeting and identify yourself; listen and take notes; speak clearly and politely; offer a message if the person is not available; thank the caller and end politely."),
            PQ("What is a PABX and what are its advantages?", "A PABX is a private automatic branch exchange, a switchboard that links the organization's extensions to each other and to outside lines. Advantages: internal calls are cheap or free; calls can be transferred; fewer outside lines are needed; features such as voicemail and call-forwarding are possible."),
            PQ("Give three disadvantages of using the telephone for business communication.", "No permanent written record unless notes are made; possible misunderstanding because of poor lines or accents; long-distance or mobile calls can be expensive; the call may interrupt other work; network or power failure."),
            SUM("Answer promptly, identify the organization and yourself, listen, speak clearly and take accurate messages.",
                "A telephone message records for whom, date and time, caller, number, message and action.",
                "A switchboard (PABX) links extensions and outside lines; the telephonist operates it; an intercom is for internal calls.",
                "The telephone is fast and personal but leaves no written record unless you make one."),
        ],
    ),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2.4
    lesson(
        module=M2,
        topic="Internal and external communication media",
        title="Internal and external communication media",
        slug="ofp1-internal-and-external-communication-media",
        minutes=22,
        summary="Organizations communicate inside (memos, notice boards, intercom, meetings, email) and outside (letters, telephone, email, messaging apps, fax, courier). The right medium depends on speed, cost, need for a record, confidentiality and the receiver.",
        objectives=[
            "Distinguish internal from external communication",
            "Name written, oral, visual and electronic media used in offices",
            "Compare media by speed, cost, record and privacy",
            "Choose a suitable medium for a given situation",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Communication", "Medium", "Internal communication", "External communication", "Email", "Memorandum", "Notice board", "Messaging app", "Feedback"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("A school principal wants to tell all teachers that exams start earlier. She also needs to tell parents. For teachers she may use the staff notice board and a short meeting. For parents she may send a letter, a text or WhatsApp message to class groups, and make an announcement at the gate."),
            T("The principal chose different **media** because the audiences were different. In this lesson you learn the main media and how to decide among them."),
            H("What is communication?"),
            D("**Communication** is the exchange of information, ideas or instructions between a sender and a receiver, so that the receiver understands what the sender meant. A **medium** (plural: media) is the channel that carries the message.",
              title="Definition: communication and medium",
              note="Good communication includes feedback: the receiver replies to show the message was understood."),
            D("**Internal communication** takes place between people inside the same organization (manager to staff, between departments). **External communication** takes place between the organization and people or organizations outside it (customers, suppliers, banks, government).",
              title="Internal and external communication"),
            H("Internal communication media"),
            KEY("Common internal media", "Written, oral and electronic.", cards=[
                ["Memorandum (memo)", "A short written message between staff of the same organization. It gives a record and is cheap (see Module 6)."],
                ["Notice board", "Displays notices for all staff. Cheap but not everyone reads it."],
                ["Meetings", "Face-to-face discussion that allows questions and decisions. Takes time and cost of staff time."],
                ["Intercom / internal telephone", "Quick spoken contact between extensions. No written record."],
                ["Email and messaging groups", "Fast, cheap, can reach many people and attach documents. Needs devices and network."],
                ["Reports and circulars", "Written information for many staff, kept in the file."],
            ]),
            H("External communication media"),
            KEY("Common external media", "Match each with typical use.", cards=[
                ["Business letter", "Formal, creates a record, suitable for contracts, complaints and formal requests. Slow, costs postage."],
                ["Telephone and mobile call", "Fast, personal, immediate answer. No written record. Cost depends on the call."],
                ["Email", "Very fast, low cost, attachments and copies, written record. Needs internet and email address."],
                ["Messaging apps (for example WhatsApp)", "Fast and informal, widely used by small businesses and customers; messages and photos can be kept as evidence. Not suitable for formal or confidential documents unless the organization allows it."],
                ["Postal and courier services", "Carry documents and parcels when the original paper is needed (see next lesson)."],
                ["Fax", "Sends a copy of a page by telephone line. Largely replaced by email and scanned attachments but still used in some offices."],
                ["Notices, posters, radio, newspapers, social media", "Reach many members of the public in advertising and public announcements."],
            ]),
            T("Some books also group media as **written**, **oral**, **visual** (charts, posters) and **audio-visual** (video, presentations). Learn the grouping your textbook uses."),
            H("Choosing the right medium"),
            KEY("Questions to ask", "Check these before sending.", cards=[
                ["Speed", "Does the message need to arrive today?"],
                ["Cost", "Postage, phone credit, data, and staff time."],
                ["Record", "Do we need a written copy or proof?"],
                ["Privacy", "Is the content confidential?"],
                ["Receiver", "Who will receive it, and which medium can they use (network, literacy, language)?"],
                ["Distance and number", "One person or many; near or far?"],
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 1: choosing a medium", intro="A company must send a signed contract to a supplier in another town, and the supplier needs the original paper. Which medium is best?", steps=[
                S("Is a record or original needed?", text="Yes. The original signed paper is required."),
                S("Is it urgent?", text="The company wants it within two days."),
                S("Decide", text="Use a courier service (or registered post) because it carries the physical document with tracking. An email copy can be sent as well."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: choosing a medium", intro="A manager wants to remind 40 staff about a meeting tomorrow at 9:00. Which medium?", steps=[
                S("Many receivers, short message, urgent", text="Individual letters would be slow and expensive."),
                S("Options", text="Email or a messaging group for all staff, plus a notice on the board."),
                S("Decide", text="Send a message to the staff group and put a notice on the board; ask for confirmation of receipt if needed."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Saying that one medium is always best", "Each medium has advantages and disadvantages. Say why one is best for the situation given."),
            MISTAKE("Using an informal medium for a formal matter", "A dismissal, a contract or a legal notice should not be sent by a quick informal message. Use a formal letter, with a record."),
            TIP("To compare media, use headings: speed, cost, record, privacy. A neat comparison earns more marks than a long paragraph."),
            QUIZ("Which is an example of internal communication?",
                 ["A letter to a customer", "A memo from the manager to the accounts department", "An advertisement in a newspaper", "A quotation sent to a supplier"], 1,
                 "Internal communication stays inside the organization, between its staff and departments.",
                 hint="Who are the sender and receiver?",
                 feedback=["The customer is outside.", "", "Advertisements go to the public.", "The supplier is outside."]),
            QUIZ("Which medium gives a permanent written record, is quick and can carry attachments?",
                 ["A telephone call", "Email", "A shout across the room", "A rumour"], 1,
                 "Email can be saved and filed, travels in minutes and can carry documents.",
                 hint="Think of something both written and fast.",
                 feedback=["A call leaves no written record.", "", "Spoken, no record.", "Unreliable and not a proper medium."]),
            PQ("Distinguish between internal and external communication, giving one medium for each.", "Internal communication is between people in the same organization, for example a memo. External communication is between the organization and outsiders, for example a business letter."),
            PQ("State three advantages and two disadvantages of email as a business medium.", "Advantages: fast; low cost; attachments and copies; written record. Disadvantages: needs internet and power; messages can be hacked or misdirected; not everyone checks email regularly."),
            PQ("A manager wants to give a formal warning to an employee. Which medium would you recommend and why?", "A formal letter or memo, handed to the employee and copied to the file. It gives a clear record, is serious in tone and can be used as evidence later."),
            SUM("Communication is sending a message and getting understanding; the channel is the medium.",
                "Internal media: memos, notice boards, meetings, intercom, email. External media: letters, phone, email, messaging apps, courier, advertisements.",
                "Choose the medium by speed, cost, need for a record, privacy and the receiver.",
                "Formal matters need formal, recorded media."),
        ],
    ),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2.5
    lesson(
        module=M2,
        topic="Postal and courier services",
        title="Postal and courier services",
        slug="ofp1-postal-and-courier-services",
        minutes=20,
        summary="Organizations send letters and parcels through the post office or through private courier companies. Services differ in speed, cost, proof of delivery and security, so the sender chooses according to the item sent.",
        objectives=[
            "Describe the main postal services: ordinary, registered, express, parcel, PO Box",
            "Explain what a courier service is and how it differs from the post",
            "Compare postal and courier services",
            "Choose a suitable service for a given item",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Post office", "Ordinary mail", "Registered mail", "Express mail", "Parcel post", "PO Box", "Courier", "Proof of delivery", "Tracking"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("A school sends exam scripts to another town. A company sends a signed contract to Douala. A student's family sends a parcel of food to a relative studying abroad. "
              "Each item needs a way to travel, and the best way depends on how fast, how safe and how costly it should be."),
            T("In this lesson you learn what postal and courier services offer. We do not quote prices here because rates change: always check the current rates and conditions with the post office or the courier company."),
            H("Postal services"),
            D("The **post** is a public service that collects, carries and delivers letters and parcels. In Cameroon the national postal operator is Cameroon Postal Services (Campost). Services and conditions can change, so check the current list with the post office.",
              title="Definition: postal service"),
            KEY("Typical postal services", "Know what each is for.", cards=[
                ["Ordinary (surface) mail", "Cheapest basic service for letters and cards. No special protection or proof of delivery; may be slow."],
                ["Registered mail", "The sender gets a receipt as proof of posting, the item is recorded at each stage, and the receiver signs on delivery. Used for important documents and valuables."],
                ["Express mail", "A faster, priority service for urgent items, usually with higher cost and sometimes tracking."],
                ["Parcel post", "Carries parcels up to a stated size and weight. Check the rules for packing, size and weight."],
                ["Airmail", "Faster than surface mail for items going abroad."],
                ["Post office box (PO Box)", "A numbered box rented at the post office. Mail is collected by the renter. Useful where there are no street addresses or for privacy."],
                ["Poste restante", "Mail held at a post office for a traveller to collect with identity."],
                ["Money services", "Many post offices also send money orders or transfers. Check the current rules."],
            ]),
            T("Mail that is sent with proof of posting is a useful legal record. For example, a **certificate of posting** proves a letter was handed in on a given date, although it does not prove delivery. Check the current names of services with your local post office."),
            H("Courier services"),
            D("A **courier service** is a private company that collects items from the sender, carries them to the receiver, often door to door, and usually offers speed, tracking and proof of delivery.",
              title="Definition: courier service"),
            T("Courier companies serve cities and often handle international delivery. Many also operate through agents in towns. Some travel agencies and transport companies carry parcels between towns as part of their business, and local motorcycle riders and van drivers deliver within a town. "
              "Quality and safety vary, so choose a reliable provider and keep the receipt."),
            KEY("Postal service and courier compared", "Learn these pairs.", cards=[
                ["Postal service", "Public service with wide coverage, including small places. Cost is often lower for ordinary items. Delivery times and tracking may be less certain."],
                ["Courier service", "Private company. Usually faster and more reliable, with door-to-door collection, tracking and signatures. Often costs more and may serve fewer remote places."],
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 1: choosing a service", intro="A firm must send a signed tender document (valuable and urgent) to a ministry in another city, arriving in two days with proof of delivery. Which service?", steps=[
                S("List the needs", text="Speed (two days), safety, proof of delivery."),
                S("Match to services", text="Ordinary mail has no proof and may be slow. Registered mail has proof. Express mail or a courier is faster."),
                S("Decide", text="Use a courier (or express registered mail) and keep the receipt and tracking number."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: choosing a service", intro="A school sends 50 circulars (not urgent) to local parents. Which is the cheapest suitable method?", steps=[
                S("List the needs", text="Low cost, no urgency, no proof needed."),
                S("Options", text="Ordinary mail or delivery by pupils or a messenger."),
                S("Decide", text="Delivery by pupils or ordinary mail is cheapest. A group message can also be used where parents have phones."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Using ordinary mail for valuable documents", "Ordinary mail has no tracking or proof. Use registered post or a courier for anything important, and keep the receipt."),
            MISTAKE("Quoting prices from memory", "Rates and services change. In an answer, say \"check current rates\" and concentrate on the type of service."),
            TIP("Compare postal and courier services under speed, cost, security, tracking and coverage. A short table-style answer scores well."),
            QUIZ("Which postal service gives the sender a receipt and records the item at each stage?",
                 ["Ordinary mail", "Registered mail", "A notice board", "A PO Box"], 1,
                 "Registered mail is recorded and signed for, giving proof of posting and delivery.",
                 hint="Which word means \"entered in a record\"?",
                 feedback=["Ordinary mail has no such records.", "", "A notice board is not a mail service.", "A PO Box is a place to receive mail, not a delivery guarantee."]),
            QUIZ("A courier company usually differs from the post office because it is...",
                 ["a private firm offering door-to-door and tracking", "free of charge", "owned by the government", "unable to deliver parcels"], 0,
                 "Couriers are private firms, often faster and with tracking. They normally cost more.",
                 hint="Think about what you pay extra for.",
                 feedback=["", "Courier services are paid.", "Most are private, not government posts.", "They do deliver parcels."]),
            PQ("Name four services offered by a post office.", "Ordinary mail, registered mail, express mail, parcel post, airmail, PO Box rental, poste restante, money transfer. Any four. (Check current services.)"),
            PQ("State two advantages of using a courier service rather than ordinary mail.", "Faster delivery; door-to-door collection and delivery; tracking and proof of delivery; better security for valuable items."),
            PQ("An accountant must send a company's original certificate to a bank in another town. Which service would you choose and why?", "Registered post or a courier, because the document is valuable and the bank may need proof of delivery. Keep the receipt or tracking number."),
            SUM("The post (Campost in Cameroon) offers ordinary, registered, express, parcel and PO Box services; check current rules and rates.",
                "Registered mail gives proof of posting and delivery. Express mail is faster.",
                "Couriers are private firms that collect and deliver door to door with tracking, usually at higher cost.",
                "Choose the service by urgency, value, proof needed and cost."),
        ],
    ),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3.1
    lesson(
        module=M3,
        topic="Incoming mail",
        title="Handling incoming mail step by step",
        slug="ofp1-incoming-mail",
        minutes=22,
        summary="Incoming mail is handled in a fixed order: receive, sort, open, extract and check, date-stamp, record (valuables in the remittance book) and distribute. Each step protects documents and money and makes sure the right person gets each item.",
        objectives=[
            "List the steps in handling incoming mail in the correct order",
            "Explain what is done at each step and why",
            "Know which mail is not opened, and how to deal with enclosures and valuables",
            "Apply the procedure to a short case",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Incoming mail", "Sorting", "Opening", "Extracting", "Enclosure", "Date-stamp", "Remittance", "Distribution", "Mail room"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("Every morning a bundle of letters, parcels and packets arrives at a company: invoices, orders, cheques, bank statements, advertisements and personal letters for staff. "
              "If a cheque is lost or an order is delivered late, the company loses money or a customer. A careful routine in the **mail room** (or at the registry or reception) prevents this."),
            D("**Incoming mail** is all the post and deliveries that arrive at an organization from outside: letters, parcels, packets, cheques, invoices, and also email and messages that are handled by a similar routine.",
              title="Definition: incoming mail"),
            IMG("f5ofp1-incoming-flow.svg", alt="A flow chart of seven numbered boxes joined by arrows: receive the mail, sort, open, extract and check, date-stamp, record, distribute. A note says valuables are entered in the remittance book.",
                caption="Figure 1. The path of incoming mail. Valuables are also entered in the remittance book."),
            H("The steps"),
            KEY("1 to 3: receive, sort and open", "Start with care.", cards=[
                ["1. Receive", "Accept the mail from the postman, courier, PO Box or messenger. Check registered or special items and sign for them if required."],
                ["2. Sort", "Separate by type and destination: items for departments or named people; private/personal mail; registered items; parcels; wrongly addressed mail."],
                ["3. Open", "Open ordinary business mail with a letter opener or machine, keeping the envelope intact. Mail marked \"Private\", \"Personal\" or \"Confidential\" is not opened by the mail room; it goes unopened to the named person."],
            ]),
            KEY("4 to 7: extract, stamp, record and distribute", "Finish with accuracy.", cards=[
                ["4. Extract and check", "Remove all contents and check that the enclosures listed are present (cheque, form, invoice). Look inside the envelope until it is empty. Note any missing item on the letter and tell the sender if needed."],
                ["5. Date-stamp", "Stamp the date (and sometimes the time) of arrival on each letter. This shows when it was received and helps to follow up late replies."],
                ["6. Record", "Enter details in the incoming mail register. Money, cheques and other valuables are entered in the remittance book (next lesson)."],
                ["7. Distribute", "Send each item to the right person or department promptly, using messengers, pigeon-holes or a trolley. Urgent items go first."],
            ]),
            T("The **enclosures** are the things sent inside the letter. A letter may say \"I enclose a cheque\" and the clerk marks the letter with a note or the abbreviation Encl. if the cheque is present. "
              "If the enclosure is missing, the clerk writes \"enclosure missing\" and tells the supervisor. If the sender's address is only on the envelope, the envelope is pinned to the letter."),
            T("In many small offices and schools the same person does all the steps. In large organizations a mail room team does them. Today much correspondence arrives by email or messaging apps, and the same ideas apply: log them, forward them to the right person and keep valuable ones safe."),
            EX("Worked example 1: sorting", intro="The mail bundle at an invented school contains: (a) a letter for the bursar, (b) an envelope marked Personal for the principal, (c) a registered packet, (d) a parcel of books for the library. What should the clerk do with each?", steps=[
                S("(a) Business letter", text="Open it, extract, date-stamp, record and send to the bursar."),
                S("(b) Personal envelope", text="Do not open it. Pass it unopened to the principal."),
                S("(c) Registered packet", text="Sign for it, record it, open it carefully and check the contents; enter any valuables in the remittance book."),
                S("(d) Parcel", text="Record it and send it to the library."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: a missing enclosure", intro="A letter says \"I enclose a cheque for the order\". After careful extraction, no cheque is found. What should the clerk do?", steps=[
                S("Check again", text="Look inside the envelope and in the other papers."),
                S("Record the problem", text="Write \"cheque not enclosed\" on the letter, with the date and initials."),
                S("Report", text="Tell the supervisor, who contacts the sender."),
                S("Keep the envelope", text="Pin it to the letter as evidence of how the letter came."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Throwing away the envelope too early", "The envelope may carry the sender's address, the postmark date or a registered number. Check it is empty, then keep it if there is any doubt."),
            MISTAKE("Opening mail marked Private or Personal", "Do not open it. Pass it to the person named, who will decide what is business and what is personal."),
            TIP("Use the memory phrase: Receive, Sort, Open, Extract, Stamp, Record, Distribute. In an exam, state the steps in order and explain each in a few words."),
            QUIZ("Which step comes immediately after opening the mail?",
                 ["Distribution", "Extracting and checking the contents", "Dispatch", "Franking"], 1,
                 "After opening, the contents are removed and checked, including any enclosures.",
                 hint="You cannot stamp or record a letter that is still inside its envelope.",
                 feedback=["Distribution is the last step.", "", "Dispatch is for outgoing mail.", "Franking is for outgoing mail."]),
            QUIZ("A letter marked \"Personal\" arrives. The mail clerk should...",
                 ["open it and read it", "pass it unopened to the person named", "throw it away", "give it to any visitor"], 1,
                 "Private and personal mail is not opened by the mail room.",
                 hint="Whose letter is it?",
                 feedback=["That would invade the person's privacy.", "", "That wastes someone's mail.", "That is a security risk."]),
            PQ("List in order the seven steps in handling incoming mail.", "Receive; sort; open; extract and check; date-stamp; record (valuables in the remittance book); distribute."),
            PQ("Why is incoming mail date-stamped?", "To show the date (and time) of arrival, which proves when the letter was received, helps to measure delay, and supports follow-up and legal proof if there is a dispute."),
            PQ("A letter states that two documents are enclosed, but only one is found. Describe the clerk's action.", "Check the envelope again, mark the letter that one enclosure is missing, record the date, tell the supervisor so that the sender is contacted, and pin the envelope to the letter."),
            SUM("Incoming mail: receive, sort, open, extract and check, date-stamp, record, distribute.",
                "Private or personal mail is not opened by the mail room.",
                "Check every enclosure; note anything missing; keep the envelope if needed.",
                "Valuables are entered in the remittance book, and the same steps apply to electronic correspondence."),
        ],
    ),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3.2
    lesson(
        module=M3,
        topic="Outgoing mail",
        title="Handling outgoing mail: collecting to dispatch",
        slug="ofp1-outgoing-mail",
        minutes=22,
        summary="Outgoing mail is collected, checked and signed, folded and inserted, addressed, weighed, franked or stamped, entered in the dispatch book and sent. A franking machine prints postage in place of stamps, and a copy of every letter is kept.",
        objectives=[
            "List the steps in handling outgoing mail in order",
            "Explain folding, inserting, weighing and franking",
            "Describe the franking machine and its advantages over stamps",
            "State the purpose of the dispatch book and fill one in",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Outgoing mail", "Folding and inserting", "Weighing", "Franking machine", "Postage", "Dispatch book", "Enclosure", "Copy"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("At 3 p.m. a company clerk collects letters from every desk. One letter has no signature. Another is addressed to \"Mr Fon, Bamenda\" with no street or PO Box. "
              "A third needs a cheque inside it, but the cheque is still on the accountant's desk. If these problems are not noticed before posting, the company looks careless, and money may be delayed."),
            D("**Outgoing mail** is all letters, documents and parcels that leave the organization for outsiders. The routine for dealing with them is the **outgoing mail procedure**.",
              title="Definition: outgoing mail"),
            IMG("f5ofp1-outgoing-flow.svg", alt="A flow chart of seven numbered boxes joined by arrows: collect, check and sign, fold and insert, weigh, frank or stamp, enter in the dispatch book, dispatch. A note says to keep a copy of every letter sent.",
                caption="Figure 1. The path of outgoing mail."),
            H("The steps"),
            KEY("Steps 1 to 4", "Prepare the item.", cards=[
                ["1. Collect", "Collect letters at a fixed time from every department, in a tray or basket, so that nothing is left behind."],
                ["2. Check and sign", "Check that each letter is signed, the address is complete, the enclosures are ready and the copy has been kept (carbon, photocopy or electronic file)."],
                ["3. Fold and insert", "Fold the letter neatly to fit the envelope (for a standard window envelope, fold so the address shows). Insert the letter and its enclosures, address the envelope and seal it. Mechanical machines can fold and insert many letters."],
                ["4. Weigh", "Weigh the item on scales to find the postage. Overweight items may be sent with too little postage, and the receiver may be asked to pay the difference."],
            ]),
            KEY("Steps 5 to 7", "Finish and send.", cards=[
                ["5. Frank or stamp", "Put postage on the envelope. Using stamps is simple. Using a franking machine saves time and prints the amount directly (see below)."],
                ["6. Enter in the dispatch book", "Record each item sent: date, addressee, address, item, method (ordinary, registered or courier) and postage."],
                ["7. Dispatch", "Hand the mail to the post office, courier or collector, or put it in the post box at the agreed time. Registered items are handed over at the counter and the receipt kept."],
            ]),
            H("The franking machine"),
            D("A **franking machine** prints on the envelope an impression showing that postage has been paid, in place of an adhesive stamp. The user buys a certain credit for the machine from the postal service, and the machine prints the correct amount and date and subtracts it from the credit. Rules differ between countries, so check with the postal service.",
              title="Definition: franking machine"),
            KEY("Franking machine v. stamps", "Benefits of the machine.", cards=[
                ["Advantages", "Saves time, since there is no sticking; prevents theft of stamps; shows the exact amount and date; keeps a record of postage spent; can print a small advertisement or slogan."],
                ["Limits", "The machine costs money; it is useful only where there are many letters; and it must be kept secure because it holds credit."],
            ]),
            H("The dispatch book"),
            D("A **dispatch book** (also called a postage book) is a record of all items sent out. It shows what was sent, to whom, when, by what method and at what cost, and it is used to check postage spent.",
              title="Definition: dispatch book"),
            CODE("Date    To                 Address        Item        Method    Weight  Postage  Ref\n"
                 "------  -----------------  -------------  ----------  --------  ------  -------  -----\n"
                 "3 Jun   Ako Supplies       PO Box 12      Order 41    Ordinary  40 g    (rate)   D/17\n"
                 "3 Jun   Bank branch        Town centre    Statement   Registered 60 g   (rate)   D/18\n"
                 "4 Jun   Ndip & Co (invented)  Buea        Contract    Courier   120 g   (rate)   D/19",
                 "text", title="A dispatch book (invented entries; rates left blank, check the current rates)"),
            EX("Worked example 1: order of tasks", intro="Put these tasks in the right order: weigh, dispatch, collect, frank, fold and insert, enter in the dispatch book, check and sign.", steps=[
                S("Start", text="Collect the letters from the departments."),
                S("Prepare", text="Check and sign, fold and insert."),
                S("Calculate postage", text="Weigh the item, then frank it (or stamp it)."),
                S("Finish", text="Enter in the dispatch book, then dispatch. Order: collect, check and sign, fold and insert, weigh, frank, enter in the dispatch book, dispatch."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: checking a letter before posting", intro="A letter to a supplier states \"I enclose cheque no. 205\" but only the letter is in the envelope. What should the mail clerk do?", steps=[
                S("Stop", text="Do not seal or frank the envelope."),
                S("Find the cause", text="Ask the sender or accounts whether the cheque is ready."),
                S("Enclose and re-check", text="Insert the cheque, re-check the address and then go on with weighing and franking."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Posting a letter without keeping a copy", "If the letter is lost or disputed, there is no evidence of what was said. Always keep a copy (file copy)."),
            MISTAKE("Estimating the weight", "Guessing may lead to too much or too little postage. Weigh the envelope with its contents on scales."),
            TIP("Do not quote postal rates in your answers. Write \"check the current rates\". Examiners want the procedure and the reasons."),
            QUIZ("Which step comes immediately before franking?",
                 ["Weighing", "Collecting", "Distribution", "Date-stamping"], 0,
                 "The weight decides the postage, so weighing comes before franking.",
                 hint="You must know how heavy the item is before you can pay for it.",
                 feedback=["", "Collecting is the first step.", "Distribution belongs to incoming mail.", "Date-stamping belongs to incoming mail."]),
            QUIZ("The dispatch book is used to record...",
                 ["items sent out", "items received", "staff salaries", "stock in the store"], 0,
                 "Dispatch means sending out, so the dispatch book records outgoing items.",
                 hint="Think of the word dispatch.",
                 feedback=["", "Items received are in the incoming mail register.", "Pay is in payroll records.", "Stock is in the stock record."]),
            PQ("List in order the main steps in handling outgoing mail.", "Collect; check and sign; fold and insert; weigh; frank or stamp; enter in the dispatch book; dispatch (and keep a copy)."),
            PQ("State three advantages of a franking machine.", "Saves time because no stamps need sticking; stamps cannot be stolen; shows exact amount and date; records postage used; can print a slogan. Any three."),
            PQ("What information would you enter in a dispatch book?", "Date; name of addressee; address; description of item; method of dispatch (ordinary, registered, courier); weight; postage; reference or receipt number."),
            SUM("Outgoing mail: collect, check and sign, fold and insert, weigh, frank or stamp, enter in the dispatch book, dispatch.",
                "Weigh the item to find the correct postage; guesses cost money or cause delays.",
                "A franking machine prints postage and saves time; check the postal rules for use.",
                "The dispatch book records what was sent, to whom, when, how and at what cost; keep a copy of each letter."),
        ],
    ),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3.3
    lesson(
        module=M3,
        topic="Mail records and valuables",
        title="Mail records and the remittance book",
        slug="ofp1-mail-records-and-valuables",
        minutes=20,
        summary="Mail rooms keep records: the incoming mail register, the remittance book for money and cheques received, the dispatch book for items sent, and a delivery book signed by receivers. These records give control, proof and a way to trace missing items.",
        objectives=[
            "Name the main mail records and what each shows",
            "Explain what a remittance is and how it is recorded",
            "Complete entries in a remittance book and total them",
            "Describe controls that protect valuables in the mail",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Incoming mail register", "Remittance", "Remittance book", "Crossed cheque", "Delivery book", "Dispatch book", "Control", "Audit trail"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("A company's customer says, \"I posted a cheque to you last week.\" The company says, \"We never received it.\" Who is right? "
              "With good mail records, the company can look at the day the mail was received and see if the cheque was entered. Without records, neither side can prove anything."),
            T("This lesson covers the records kept in the mail room and, in particular, how money and cheques arriving by post are recorded."),
            H("The main mail records"),
            KEY("Records and what they do", "Know the name, the content and the purpose.", cards=[
                ["Incoming mail register", "Lists items received: date, sender, type of item, department or person to whom it was sent. Shows that every item arrived and was passed on."],
                ["Remittance book", "Lists money and cheques received by post. Each entry gives date, sender, amount, type of payment and reference. Used to check that all money reaches the cashier."],
                ["Dispatch (postage) book", "Lists items sent: date, addressee, method, postage. Used to control postage costs and to prove that items were sent."],
                ["Delivery (receipt) book", "Staff sign when they receive their mail from the mail room. Proves the item was handed over."],
                ["Registered post receipts", "Slips from the post office that prove an item was handed in, with a reference number."],
            ]),
            H("Remittances"),
            D("A **remittance** is money sent to an organization by post or other means to pay for goods or services. It may be cash (rarely sent by post and risky), a cheque, a postal order or money order, a bank transfer or a mobile money transfer.",
              title="Definition: remittance",
              note="Cash by post is risky. Organizations advise customers to use cheques, transfers or other traceable methods."),
            D("A **remittance book** (cash-received book) is the record of every remittance found in the incoming mail. It is kept by the person who opens the mail, and it is checked later against the cashier's record.",
              title="Definition: remittance book"),
            T("The steps: two people (or one person and a supervisor) open the mail where money is likely; the remittance is listed immediately; the letter is marked with the entry number and the sum; "
              "the money is passed to the cashier against a signature; and the cashier issues a receipt to the sender."),
            CODE("No.  Date    From                 Type        Amount (FCFA)  Passed to cashier\n"
                 "---  ------  -------------------  ----------  -------------  -----------------\n"
                 "1    9 Mar   Customer A (invented) Cheque             45 000  signed, 11:00\n"
                 "2    9 Mar   Customer B (invented) Money order        20 000  signed, 11:00\n"
                 "3    9 Mar   Customer C (invented) Cheque             87 500  signed, 11:00\n"
                 "                                   Total            152 500",
                 "text", title="A remittance book page (invented figures for practice)"),
            T("Two other points. Cheques received are normally **crossed** (two parallel lines across the face), which means the cheque must be paid into a bank account and cannot be cashed at the counter; "
              "an incoming cheque is not cashed by the mail clerk but passed to the cashier. Second, the amounts in the remittance book must agree with the cashier's cash book and the bank. If they do not, there is an error or loss, and it must be found."),
            EX("Worked example 1: total the remittances", intro="From the invented remittance book above, find the total of the three remittances and the value of the cheques only.", steps=[
                S("Add the three amounts", text="45 000 + 20 000 + 87 500 = 152 500 FCFA."),
                S("Cheques only", text="The cheques are entries 1 and 3: 45 000 + 87 500 = 132 500 FCFA."),
                S("Check", text="132 500 + 20 000 = 152 500, so the totals agree."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: a difference", intro="The cashier's records for the day show 132 500 FCFA received from the mail, but the remittance book shows 152 500 FCFA. What should be done?", steps=[
                S("Find the difference", text="152 500 - 132 500 = 20 000 FCFA."),
                S("Look for the cause", text="Entry 2 (the money order) is exactly 20 000 FCFA, so check whether it was passed to the cashier and signed for."),
                S("Report", text="Tell the supervisor at once and do not change any figure without authority."),
                S("Prevent", text="Ensure each remittance is signed for by the cashier when passed on."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Leaving remittances loose on the desk", "Money and cheques must be recorded and passed on at once, with a signature. Loose items are easy to lose or steal."),
            MISTAKE("Cashing a received cheque in the mail room", "The mail clerk records it and passes it to the cashier. Only the cashier or the bank handles the money."),
            TIP("A good answer on controls: record on receipt, two people opening mail, immediate passing to the cashier against signature, lock valuables in a safe, and regular checks of records."),
            QUIZ("A record of cheques and money received by post is kept in the...",
                 ["remittance book", "dispatch book", "visitors' register", "minute book"], 0,
                 "The remittance book is for money received through the mail.",
                 hint="Remittance means money sent to you.",
                 feedback=["", "The dispatch book is for items sent out.", "The visitors' register records visitors.", "Minutes record decisions at meetings."]),
            QUIZ("A remittance book shows these entries: 12 500, 30 000 and 7 500 FCFA. What is the total?",
                 ["50 000 FCFA", "49 000 FCFA", "42 500 FCFA", "55 000 FCFA"], 0,
                 "12 500 + 30 000 = 42 500, and 42 500 + 7 500 = 50 000.",
                 hint="Add the first two, then add the third.",
                 feedback=["", "This is 1 000 too small.", "This leaves out the 7 500.", "This adds 5 000 too much."]),
            PQ("What is a remittance and where is it recorded when it arrives by post?", "A remittance is money, such as a cheque or money order, sent to the organization to pay for goods or services. It is recorded in the remittance book when the mail is opened, and passed to the cashier."),
            PQ("State three controls that protect money received through the mail.", "Record it at once in the remittance book; open mail in the presence of a second person or supervisor; pass the money to the cashier against a signature; keep valuables in a safe; compare the remittance book with the cashier's record."),
            PQ("Remittances of 18 000, 52 500 and 9 500 FCFA were entered. Find the total.", "18 000 + 52 500 + 9 500 = 80 000 FCFA."),
            SUM("Mail records: incoming register, remittance book, dispatch book, delivery book and receipts.",
                "A remittance is money sent by post. It is recorded at once in the remittance book.",
                "The cashier signs for what is passed on, and the remittance book is checked against the cash book.",
                "Controls: two people, immediate recording, signatures, a safe and regular checks."),
        ],
    ),
]
